{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"blizzardly-uncouth-spittlebug","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Surfaced by the 2026-08-05/06 codebase-health audit and scoped as its own epic rather than folded\ninto the janitor cleanup. Audit record: `.joe-janitor/report-2026-08-06.md`.\n\n## Why\n\nThe Jira reconciler's user-facing surface leaks internal mechanics and couples rebar core to GitHub\nActions. Six verbs today (`reconcile`, `jira-onboard`, `bridge-probe`, `bridge-status`,\n`bridge-fsck`, `purge-bridge`), of which two are dead: `bridge-status`'s producer has never existed\nin this repo (the reader was a knowing tombstone at extraction), and `purge-bridge` has five\ncommits, none of them feature or bug work, and no caller anywhere.\n\n`--mode`'s five values cannot be explained without internals because the enum FUSES two concepts —\n\"how much may this run change?\" and \"is the bridge allowed to run at all?\". Splitting them into\n`--max-changes` and `pause`/`resume` is what makes the rest of the surface writable in plain\nlanguage.\n\nrebar core shells out to `gh` for a safety precondition (`fsck_repair.py:278`), which is a\nportability violation: a client without GitHub Actions gets a destructive-repair guard that\nsilently does nothing. Meanwhile ~185 lines of logic live in the GHA workflow — including a\n106-line CAS push loop that re-implements `_store/push.py` and has diverged from it in both\ndirections.\n\n## Decisions already taken\n\n- Noun is **`bridge`** — provider-neutral (names the mechanism, not the vendor), zero collision,\n  near-zero migration cost, already the repo's word (`BRIDGE_ALERT`, `bridge_state/`).\n  `jira` was rejected because other trackers are planned.\n- **`reconcile` is retired with NO alias.** The defaults invert (`reconcile` meant dry-run via a\n  wrapper; `bridge sync` mutates), so aliasing would be a silent behaviour change.\n- `bridge fsck` is kept; the earlier `check` suggestion is dropped.\n- **`status` is canonical.** The heartbeat canary consumes it on a schedule and escalates via\n  tickets, rather than being a parallel alarm.\n- Exit codes collapse to **0/1/2**; benign outcomes (busy, reschedule, paused) exit 0 with a message.\n- Flags survive only if their help text can be written without internals, and only if justified by\n  CI, local, or troubleshooting use. Ten candidates reduced to three.\n- MCP and library get corresponding `bridge` surfaces — rebar's philosophy is three interfaces for\n  shared functionality.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar bridge <verb>` is the single surface: setup, doctor/fsck, status, preview, sync, pause, resume\n- [ ] `reconcile`, `bridge-status` and `purge-bridge` are gone, with release notes\n- [ ] Every shipped flag's help text is written without internals and is justified by CI, local, or troubleshooting use\n- [ ] Exit codes are 0/1/2 and the CI whitelist block is deleted\n- [ ] Pause is remote-persisted, carries a reason, and cannot wedge a mixed-version fleet\n- [ ] rebar core requires no `gh` binary for any documented operation\n- [ ] **The bridge runs locally, on GitHub Actions, and on Jenkins** — verified per the portability AC below\n- [ ] The 106-line YAML push loop is deleted and `_store/push.py` is reconciled with it\n- [ ] `docs/user-guide.md:364`'s broken `--dry-run` example cannot recur\n\n## Portability AC (verifiable in-session, not by a future adopter)\n\n- [ ] A contract test runs the entrypoint under scrubbed environments for {local, github-actions, jenkins, gitlab, bare} and passes identically; it fails if any vendor variable is read or any `gh` invocation is required\n- [ ] The same test asserts a full-depth clone succeeds and a **shallow clone is rejected with a named error** rather than proceeding (bug f193)\n- [ ] `Jenkinsfile` and `.gitlab-ci.yml` exist as REAL COMMITTED FILES that the docs reference rather than duplicate\n- [ ] The GHA artifact shipped as an example is the workflow rebar actually runs (or a reusable workflow both consume), so it is exercised every 20 minutes\n- [ ] All three configs are asserted to share one exit-code table, one secret-name set, and one timeout, each cross-checked against the code rather than hardcoded\n- [ ] The Jenkinsfile's `sh` body is asserted to be a SINGLE-QUOTED Groovy string (a double-quoted GString interpolates the secret before the shell sees it, defeating masking)\n- [ ] `.gitlab-ci.yml` validates against GitLab's official JSON schema, offline, using dependencies rebar already has\n- [ ] shellcheck runs over script bodies extracted from all three configs, failing loudly on an unparseable config\n- [ ] Uncovered risks are documented as KNOWN GAPS outside the AC, not as future adopter work","id":"2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["bridge","ux"],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Bridge redesign: one portable command surface for tracker sync"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786047237856716001,"uuid":"92d364e6-0816-4ae8-8147-7371356cd75c"}